## Why This Is Asked
Examines a practical sleep-wake path and basic data-structuring under concurrency.

## Key Concepts
- Timer wheel with fixed slots for constant-time operations
- Safe concurrent enqueue and wakeup via spinlocks
- Tick progression and wrap-around handling

## Code Example
```javascript
typedef struct Thread { int id; struct Thread *next; } Thread;

#define WHEEL_SIZE 64
typedef struct {
  Thread *buckets[WHEEL_SIZE];
  int current_tick;
  SpinLock lock;
} TimerWheel;

void sleep_ms(Thread *t, int ms, TimerWheel *w) {
  int slot = (w->current_tick + ms) % WHEEL_SIZE;
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  t->next = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = t;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
}

void timer_tick(TimerWheel *w) {
  spin_lock(&w->lock);
  int slot = w->current_tick % WHEEL_SIZE;
  Thread *cur = w->buckets[slot];
  w->buckets[slot] = NULL;
  w->current_tick++;
  spin_unlock(&w->lock);
  while (cur) { wake(cur); cur = cur->next; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for correctness under concurrent enqueues and wakes?
- What changes if ms can exceed wheel size multiple times?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical skills for implementing sleep/wake in a preemptive kernel, focusing on data structures, ISR interaction, and race conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Sleep list ordered by wake_tick
- Per-task wake_tick and state fields
- Interrupt masking or locks to avoid races between enqueue and timer
- Immediate wakeup and re-scheduling to ready queue

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo C-like snippet
typedef struct Task { int wake_tick; int state; struct Task* next; } Task;
Task* sleep_list = NULL;
void sleep(int n) {
  int w = current_tick + n;
  task->wake_tick = w;
  disable_interrupts();
  insert_ordered(&sleep_list, task, w);
  task->state = SLEEPING;
  schedule();
  enable_interrupts();
}
void timer_handler() {
  current_tick++;
  while (sleep_list && sleep_list->wake_tick <= current_tick) {
    Task* t = pop_head(&sleep_list);
    t->state = READY;
    enqueue_ready(t);
  }
}
```

